body:
Portable layout names: a layout's name — the string an author writes in `{{ template "<name>" . }}` — is the layout's layer-relative forward-slash path within its library layer: `slides/<template-name>/layout.html.tmpl` for a slide layout and `sections/<template-name>/layout.html.tmpl` for a section layout. The same string names the layout on Windows, macOS and Linux and wherever the deck or library sits on disk, so a deck checked into git composes identically on any machine. The name never contains a layer root, a `templates/` prefix, a drive letter, a backslash, or an absolute path. Because template names are unique across the local and external layers (library-layer-uniqueness), a layer-relative name is unambiguous in the merged set. A local-layer layout may include an external-layer layout by that layout's own layer-relative name; an external-layer layout naming a local-only layout is a templates error, because the external layer validates standalone (library-layer-references, templates-dir-validation). An included layout executes in the invoking layout's namespace, so its `media` helper binds to the invoking layer, and a `section` call rebinds to the target's own layer (guide-layout-composition, example-layout-composition). An error names the layout's full on-disk file path so an author can find the file; that path is for display only and is never the name.

AC:
- the name an author writes for a slide layout is `slides/<template-name>/layout.html.tmpl` and for a section layout is `sections/<template-name>/layout.html.tmpl`;
- the same name resolves on Windows, macOS and Linux and wherever the deck or library sits on disk, with no layer root, `templates/` prefix, drive letter, backslash or absolute path in it;
- a local-layer layout resolves an external-layer layout by that layout's own layer-relative name, and an external-layer layout naming a local-only layout is a templates error;
- an included layout's `media` helper resolves in the invoking layout's layer, and a `section` call resolves in the target's own layer;
- a templates error about a layout names its full on-disk file path, never its layer-relative name.
